  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nQuiet and partly sunny skies continue this Thanksgiving afternoon. \nHigh temperatures have warmed to 30s, 40s, and even low 50s across \nsouth central South Dakota. The partly sunny skies will persist for \nthe rest of today. Winds will go from light and variable to just \nslightly elevated out of the south as surface high pressure begins \nto nudge eastwards. Thus, low temperatures will be a little bit \nwarmer from what we saw this morning, only falling down to the teens \nand 20s.\n\nA weak shortwave trough will push through the forecast area during \nthe afternoon hours on Friday. This wave will bring a return to \ncloudy skies and keep high temperatures a shade lower though still \nreaching the 30s and 40s thanks to the weak warm air advection (WAA) \nfrom the wave in addition to diurnal heating. Perhaps some locations \ntowards central and south central South Dakota could see highs reach \nup to about 50F. Sprinkles to very light rain remains on the table \nwith this wave as the dendritic growth zone (DGZ) saturates. There \nwill also be some negative omega (upward motion) within the DGZ as \nwell. However, the low levels will be dry. There appears to be \nenough lift to wet bulb light rain to the surface. This light rain \nlooks to push through the area during the late morning and afternoon \nhours, leaving dry conditions for the evening timeframe. Rainfall \namounts are expected to be very light at only a few hundredths at \nthe most. \n\nAnother chance for rain will come for Saturday afternoon and evening \nas another shortwave trough passes through the region. This \nshortwave will have sufficient mid level confluence, resulting in \nstretching deformation along the thermal gradient, yielding \nfrontogenesis (FGEN) at 700 to 600 mb. Sufficient saturation will be \nin place for rain to develop from the FGEN. The best forcing and \ndynamic cooling will be south of the forecast area. Combining this \nwith afternoon highs reaching into the 40s should keep precipitation \nall rain for this event. There could be some snowflakes that mix in \non the back side of the rain later Saturday evening. Latest \nensembles show moderate to high probabilities (50-90%) for rainfall \ntotals to exceed a hundredth of an inch. Their probabilities drop to \na 25-50% chance for a quarter of an inch of rain, with the highest \nprobabilities occurring over highway-20. Though the extent of rain \nlooks to extend northwards up to at least highway-18 if not \npotentially up to I-90. This will depend on minor shifts in this \nbands path over the next 24 to 48 hours. Do have some concerns for \nthe potential of a sharper cut off on the north end of the rain as \nthe FGEN circulation may wrap in some drier air on this side of the \ncirculation. Something to keep an eye on moving forwards. \n\nSunday into the beginning of next week looks generally dry at this \ntime as west/northwest flow dominates the Northern Plains. Things \ncould change by the middle of the week as medium range guidance is \nin decent agreement in a stronger upper level wave pushing eastward \nacross the southern CONUS. This could result in stronger advections \nto give us the next chance for precipitation. Details can still \nchange so have continued with the NBM's PoPs at this time. Other \nthan precipitation chances, Sunday through the middle of next week \nlooks to remain near to above seasonable with highs in the 30s and \n40s.",
